I wrote the module but could not run it. The sandbox asked for approval to run `node`, and I did not retry. Here is the implementation, in `src/orderid.mjs`:

```js
const PREFIX = "ACM";
const ID_PATTERN = /^ACM-(\d{4})-(\d{6})$/;
const MAX_SEQUENCE = 999999;

export function nextOrderId(date, lastId) {
  if (!(date instanceof Date) || Number.isNaN(date.getTime())) {
    throw new TypeError("date must be a valid Date");
  }
  const year = date.getUTCFullYear();
  let sequence = 1;
  if (lastId !== null && lastId !== undefined) {
    const match = ID_PATTERN.exec(lastId);
    if (!match) throw new Error(`invalid order id: ${lastId}`);
    if (Number(match[1]) === year) sequence = Number(match[2]) + 1;
  }
  if (sequence > MAX_SEQUENCE) {
    throw new RangeError(`order sequence for ${year} is exhausted`);
  }
  return `${PREFIX}-${year}-${String(sequence).padStart(6, "0")}`;
}
```

Choices I made:

- **Format source:** The repo has no existing id code, so I used the format from the saved memory note: `ACM-2026-000042`. Please confirm that is still current.
- **Year:** I used the UTC year. If your orders are dated in local time, this can put an order from around New Year's into the wrong year. Say if you want local time.
- **Reset:** A `lastId` from a different year restarts the sequence at 1.
- **Errors:** A malformed `lastId` throws. So does an invalid date, and a sequence past 999999 for one year.

I have not run the code or any tests. Once you approve running `node`, I can check these cases: `null` gives `ACM-2026-000001`, `ACM-2026-000042` gives `...000043`, a `2025` id rolls over to `2026` at 1, and `ACM-2026-999999` throws.
